# Illustration colours

Use this page to choose colours for an illustration, such as a picture that explains where to put a sensor. For when to use an illustration and how to draw it, see [Illustration](illustration.html). Illustration colours do not change with the household's theme colour.

## Illustrations palette

Use the palette for the channel where the illustration will appear. For example, use [Web-app colours](colour-web-app.html) for documentation pages, dashboards and app screens, and [Social colours](colour-social.html) for social media.

Use as few colours as you can. As a rule of thumb, use 4 to 6 colours, depending on how complex the illustration is and how many elements it has.

<%= colour_copy_status %>

<%= colour_palette('illustrations') %>

Primary blue is Civica’s default theme colour, so make it the base of the illustration and use it widely. Use Accent teal if it suits the illustration and its layout. It is bold, so use it to highlight a key component. Accent blue is App only in [Web-app colours](colour-web-app.html), so use it only in an illustration for app screens. Documentation and dashboard illustrations do not use it.

In this example, an app screen illustration, Accent teal and Accent blue highlight the sensor on the Primary blue wall, where both reach 3:1.

<%= colour_example('illustration-colour-applying', callout: false) %>

### Colour pairing

Choose colours that complement each other, with enough contrast to tell the key elements apart and keep a clear visual hierarchy.

Use different shades of one colour to separate the foreground from the background, so that the image does not look flat.

<%= colour_example('illustration-colour-pairing', callout: false) %>

### Using contrast

Contrasting colours make an illustration more appealing and easier to see.

Test the illustration with a colour blindness simulator, to check that people with a colour vision deficiency do not lose important information.

A warm colour beside a cool one, such as yellow beside blue, makes a focal point that draws attention to the action. Keep the blues of the palette dominant.

In this example, the person in yellow stands in front of the dark window and the person in blue in front of the White wall. The greyscale copy is a quick check that the scene still reads without colour; it does not replace a colour blindness simulator.

<%= colour_example('illustration-colour-contrast', callout: false) %>
